;; TOOL-RFC-004, the verified core of the archive-disposition gate.
;;
;; The RFC is docs/design/tool-rfc-004-doc-archive.md. This module is section
;; 7's THIRD instrument: a contract that references neither implementation and
;; therefore outlives the reference script, which section 8 deletes.
;;
;; WHY THIS IS ITS OWN MODULE AND NOT PART OF THE PORT. A `def` cannot call a
;; sibling `def` in the same module: the failure is at check time, before
;; verification, and no ordering escapes it. Cross-module calls to an
;; already-verified callee do work, leaf-first. So a proved core's module split
;; is forced by the language rather than chosen for tidiness, and the port
;; imports this.
;;
;; THE CONTRACT IS INPUT-SIDE AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE. It constrains
;; `result` per constructor of the INPUT, which is the classify.llmll shape.
;; THE TYPES FORCE THIS, and nothing else does. `side-of` maps `Disposition` to
;; `Side`, which are two different sum types, so there is no identity-shaped
;; post to write: `(post (= result d))` is a TYPE ERROR here. Measured:
;; "type mismatch in '=': expected SpecDir | DormantDir | NoSide, got
;; Shipped | Superseded | Dropped | Deferred | UnknownD".
;;
;; THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY SOMETHING ELSE AND IT WAS WRONG TWICE OVER, which
;; is worth keeping because the error propagated. It said the input-side shape
;; was forced by MATCH-TERM-EQ-1, and that a post relating `result` to the
;; scrutinee VARIABLE was falsely refuted by the compiler. First: that row's
;; cause was misstated everywhere it appeared, and the real cause was the param
;; binder carrying no int-tag domain, fixed at v0.16.2. Second, and the reason
;; this file was never affected: the alternative it described is not refutable
;; here, it is ill-typed, before the fix and after it. Three records agreed on
;; the wrong cause because they were copied from each other. See
;; docs/design/tool-ll-RESTART.md section 3.
;;
;; `and` is binary here, not variadic, so the conjunction nests.
;;
;; MEASURED, not assumed: this module verifies SAFE and reports
;; `body-faithful: side-of`, so the pass is not a fallback. Misrouting a single
;; arm (Superseded -> DormantDir) is REFUTED at constraint #1. That refuting
;; case is what separates a proof from a green light: a fallback body cannot be
;; refuted, and a postcondition of `false` on one still reports SAFE.

(module adjudicate

  ;; The vocabulary of `archive-disposition`, plus the value the reference
  ;; reports as "not one of shipped|superseded|dropped|deferred". UnknownD
  ;; rather than Unknown: `check` is a reserved word in this language and
  ;; short constructor names near reserved ones have cost time before.
  (type Disposition
    (| Shipped)
    (| Superseded)
    (| Dropped)
    (| Deferred)
    (| UnknownD))

  ;; The two governed directories, plus "this value names no side", which is
  ;; the reference's first violation class rather than an error state.
  (type Side
    (| SpecDir)
    (| DormantDir)
    (| NoSide))

  ;; shipped | superseded -> shipped-design-specs/
  ;; dropped  | deferred  -> dormant-explorations/
  ;; anything else        -> no side, which the caller reports as a violation.
  (def side-of [d: Disposition] -> Side
    (post (and (and (=> (= d Shipped)    (= result SpecDir))
                    (=> (= d Superseded) (= result SpecDir)))
               (and (=> (= d Dropped)    (= result DormantDir))
                    (and (=> (= d Deferred) (= result DormantDir))
                         (=> (= d UnknownD) (= result NoSide))))))
    (match d
      ((Shipped)    SpecDir)
      ((Superseded) SpecDir)
      ((Dropped)    DormantDir)
      ((Deferred)   DormantDir)
      ((UnknownD)   NoSide))))
